The installer
=============

Design for the thing a user actually downloads. doc/shipping is the wider plan
and the constraints; this is the installer itself, in enough detail to write.

The shape is two programs. mocolinux-setup.exe is NSIS and lays down files;
mocolinux.exe --first-run brings the Linux system up and finishes installing
it. The split is not cosmetic: the second half takes twenty to forty-five
minutes over somebody's network, needs progress, cancel, retry and resume, and
speaks to the guest over a TCP console. NSIS can do none of that well, and the
launcher has to contain all of it anyway.


1. The target builds its own system
------------------------------------

The release does not ship the installed system. It ships a seed that can build
one, and the machine being installed onto runs tools/mkmanjarorootfs.sh
exactly as doc/runbook section B does today -- the seed on cobd0, a blank
image on cobd1, and the script building the second from the first.

Two things force the shape, and it is worth being clear which is which.

Something pacman-capable has to ship no matter what. mkmanjarorootfs.sh needs
a Linux with pacman and e2fsprogs already running, so it reproduces an image
and cannot create the first one; the runbook states that as its own bootstrap
problem. So there is no version of this where the download is only a kernel
and a driver. The seed is the floor.

Given that floor, the question is only what the target does with it, and the
answer is: the thing that has actually run. Growing the seed in place and
installing the desktop into it would be less work on the target -- no mke2fs,
no second image, no keyring populate -- but it introduces a step, an online
resize2fs of a live cobd0, that has never executed here. Running the script is
the path that has been executed end to end, on this hardware, repeatedly. That
decides it. The extra cost is the seed's own ~700 MB sitting beside the image,
not a second copy of it, and run-manjaro.bat already keeps the old root
attached as cobd1 for exactly this reason: if the new system will not boot,
the one that built it still will.

One script, two package sets, so there is a single source of truth for what a
MoCoLinux root filesystem is:

    mkmanjarorootfs.sh --seed <target>     base, pacman, populated keyring,
                                           e2fsprogs, networkd, hvc0 getty
    mkmanjarorootfs.sh <target>            all of the above plus the desktop

The seed is built once by whoever cuts the release. Everything the user
downloads after that comes from Manjaro's mirrors on their own machine.

Sizes, to be measured rather than promised, but the shape is:

    seed, raw          ~700 MB    what --seed produces
    seed, in installer ~250 MB    NSIS LZMA over a mostly-empty ext4
    package download   ~2.5 GB    at install time, on the user's connection
    installed          ~6 GB


2. What is in the payload
--------------------------

    colinux-daemon.exe    every daemon mode, slirp bridge included
    mocolinux.exe         launcher and first-run wizard
    linux.sys             the driver
    vmlinux               the guest kernel, ~53 MB
    root-seed.img         section 1
    vcxsrv1142\           VcXsrv 1.14.2.1, optional component (doc/shipping 5)
    LICENSE, doc\         COPYING, the runbook, third-party notices

Everything in it must load on a stock XP x64 -- no api-ms-win-*, no
kernelbase.dll, no subsystem above 5.2 -- and `make dist` refuses to package a
binary that fails that check. That is not belt and braces: it is the defect
the whole release carried until it was measured.


3. Pages
---------

    Welcome         what this is, in three sentences, and the two numbers that
                    matter: how long the setup takes and how much it downloads.

    Licence         GPL v2.

    Install to      two directories, because they have different sizes and
                    people put them on different disks.
                      Program:  %ProgramFiles%\MoCoLinux         ~60 MB
                      Linux:    C:\MoCoLinux                     see below
                    The second is validated as it is typed: NTFS, writable,
                    and enough free space. FAT32 is refused with the reason --
                    the image is larger than 4 GB and FAT32 cannot hold it,
                    which otherwise appears much later as a write error.

    What to install   Desktop (KDE Plasma, downloads ~2.5 GB, 30-45 minutes)
                      Console only (ready in about two minutes)
                      X server (VcXsrv 1.14.2.1)  [ticked if none found]
                    The console-only path is not a lesser option. It boots the
                    same system and is what somebody wants who is here for a
                    Linux shell inside XP rather than for KDE, and it turns a
                    forty-minute install into a two-minute one.

    Disk size       8 / 16 / 32 GB or custom, default 16.
                    The space is committed at creation, by SetEndOfFile rather
                    than by writing zeroes, so it costs no time but it does
                    cost the disk. Said plainly on the page, because a sparse
                    file that fails halfway through a package install is a
                    worse trade than a reservation that fails immediately.

    Installing      file copy, driver service, firewall entry.

    Setting up      hands off to the wizard -- section 5.

    Finished        with the reboot notice from section 7.


4. Before anything is written
------------------------------

Each check names what to do about it, because "setup cannot continue" with no
reason is how a user concludes the software is broken.

    64-bit Windows        ${RunningX64}, else refuse: this is an x86-64 port
                          and the driver must match the kernel.
    5.2 or later          XP x64 / Server 2003 and up.
    Administrators        the driver is a kernel service.
    NTFS on the Linux     the image exceeds FAT32's 4 GB file limit.
      directory
    Free space            image size + 60 MB, on that volume.
    CoLinuxDriver         if the service exists and is not ours -- a real
      not already there   coLinux 0.7.9 install -- stop and say so. Both want
                          the same service name and the same device object.
    Nothing running       no colinux-daemon.exe in the task list.

Deliberately not checked: whether one-core-api is present. Nothing in the
release needs it any more, and probing for it would only invite the question.


5. The wizard
--------------

One window, a stage list, a progress bar, a log pane that can be folded open,
and Cancel. It is `mocolinux.exe --first-run <inifile>`, so it is re-runnable
by hand and from the launcher's "Rebuild the Linux system".

    1  create the images    root-seed.img is laid down as shipped; root.img is
                            created empty at the chosen size with
                            SetEndOfFile, which is instant and is what
                            `fsutil file createnew` does in the runbook. The
                            guest makes the filesystem in it.
    2  driver               install the service if absent, start it, wait for
                            RUNNING. --install-driver reports success today
                            without starting anything, which is a TODO item
                            and a small fix; until it lands the wizard starts
                            the service itself rather than trusting that.
    3  boot the seed        --boot with root-seed.img on cobd0 and the blank
                            root.img on cobd1, plus --slirp and --console, as
                            children: own pipes, no inherited console handles,
                            a Job Object with KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE as backstop
                            only. `start` hands its standard handles to the
                            child whatever you do with them, and three
                            launchers in this tree have wedged that way.
    4  wait for the guest   connect to 127.0.0.1:2323 and read. Half a minute
                            is normal. Connection refused means the console
                            has not bound; silence means the guest has not
                            reached a prompt. Neither is a failure and neither
                            is reported as one. Ninety seconds is.
    5  build                the seed runs mkmanjarorootfs.sh against /dev/cobd1
                            and says what it is doing -- section 6. This is the
                            twenty to forty-five minutes.
    6  verify               section 6, against the image just built, while the
                            guest that built it is still up to do the checking.
    7  shut down            send `systemctl poweroff` and wait for the boot
                            child to exit. Never --stop: nothing else unmounts,
                            and ext4 in data=ordered journals metadata and not
                            contents, so a file written seconds earlier comes
                            back as a correctly-named inode with no data. That
                            exit is the flush -- and here it is the flush of
                            the filesystem that was just built, which is the
                            whole product.
    8  swap the disks       write the launcher's ini so root.img is cobd0 and
                            root-seed.img is cobd1, which is what
                            run-manjaro.bat does today: the system that built
                            this one stays attached as the recovery disk, able
                            to mount and repair it if it will not boot.

Console-only skips nothing structurally -- the same script builds the same
filesystem with a smaller package set, so the difference is download size and
time rather than a different path through the code.

Cancel is honest about where it is. Before stage 5 it stops and removes what
it made. During stage 5 it offers to leave root.img in place, since several
gigabytes of package cache are worth keeping for a retry.


6. What the guest says
-----------------------

The guest drives its own installation and reports on the console. The host
side only reads.

The alternative -- the wizard pushing shell to a prompt and scraping the
output -- was rejected: mkmanjarorootfs.sh already carries a comment about
layered shell quoting eating a dollar sign when it ran that way, and a
progress bar built on prompt-scraping breaks the first time a package name
contains something interesting.

So the seed carries /usr/lib/systemd/system/mocolinux-setup.service, wanted by
multi-user.target, which runs mkmanjarorootfs.sh against /dev/cobd1 and turns
its output into markers, one per line, on /dev/console:

    MOCO:STAGE <n>/<total> <text>     a step began
    MOCO:PCT <0-100>                  progress within it, where known
    MOCO:INFO <text>                  for the log pane, not the stage line
    MOCO:OK                           the whole thing succeeded
    MOCO:FAIL <code> <text>           it did not, and this is why

Anything not matching goes to the log pane verbatim. The wizard needs no shell
and no sentinel, and a marker stream is greppable in a support log afterwards.

The script's own stages already exist -- it reports each with `==>` -- so the
mapping is a table rather than new instrumentation, with an added check in
front of it:

    1/7  checking the network        one HTTP HEAD at the mirror, so an
                                     unreachable network fails here with
                                     "cannot reach the Manjaro mirror" rather
                                     than a hundred lines into pacman
    2/7  making the filesystem       mke2fs on /dev/cobd1
    3/7  downloading and installing  the long one; PCT from packages done
    4/7  populating the keyring      pacman-key --init && --populate
    5/7  ranking mirrors             pacman-mirrors
    6/7  re-verifying every package  pacman -Syu against the real keyring
    7/7  configuring                 ldconfig, locale, fstab, getty, network,
                                     user, sudo

The unit disables itself on MOCO:OK, so a second boot of the seed is an
ordinary boot. On MOCO:FAIL it stays enabled and re-runs on the next boot,
which is what makes retry work without the host having to know anything.

Verification is the guest's job too, and it is not optional -- a block-layer
bug once produced an image whose own package database was binary noise. It is
the runbook's step 5, run before the guest that built the image shuts down:

    mount /dev/cobd1 /mnt/new
    pacman --root /mnt/new -Qk        every installed file present
    test -x /mnt/new/usr/lib/systemd/systemd, /etc/fstab, the getty override
    umount, then e2fsck -n /dev/cobd1

and then the script's own last line, which is load-bearing in a way that is
easy to miss: success is printed by the unmount path and nowhere else, because
ext4 in data=ordered journals metadata and not contents. "done, unmounted
clean" is the only sentence that means the image is on the disk.

Resume is the one thing this path does not get for free. mkmanjarorootfs.sh
opens with an unconditional mke2fs -F, so a retry today throws away whatever
was downloaded. The fix is small and belongs with the --seed work: skip the
mke2fs when the target already holds a filesystem with a pacman cache in it,
and let pacman pick up where it stopped. A mirror dropping mid-download is the
likeliest failure a user will meet, and starting a 2.5 GB download again is
the difference between a retry and an abandonment.


7. The reboot
--------------

The wizard boots the guest once, so the user's first real launch is the second
boot on a loaded driver -- the case doc/runbook calls "the one that governs the
whole day". Guest RAM is freed and re-hunted in unbroken 32 MB runs every run,
so the second grinds the host with memory showing as available throughout.

An installer that ends in a reboot is unremarkable, so that is the answer for
now. The Finished page says why in one line rather than pretending it is
routine hygiene, and the launcher warns if it is started before that reboot
happens. TODO carries the fix -- allocate once per driver load -- and when it
lands both come out.


8. Registry and files
----------------------

    HKLM\SOFTWARE\MoCoLinux
        InstallDir, LinuxDir, Version
        XServer        bundled | detected | absent
        XServerPath
        Installed      the tickboxes, so the uninstaller and a repair run know
                       what was done rather than guessing

    HKLM\...\Uninstall\MoCoLinux      the usual, with EstimatedSize

    <linux>\root.img                  the guest
    <linux>\root-seed.img             the system that built it, kept as the
                                      recovery disk on cobd1
    <linux>\mocolinux.ini             the launcher's settings, plain ini
    <linux>\logs\                     setup.log, boot.log, net.log, console.log

Two directories rather than one because the binaries are 60 MB and belong with
the program, and the image is tens of gigabytes and belongs where the user has
room. The ini sits with the image, not with the program, because it names the
disks.

The firewall entry, from doc/shipping: XP prompts about vcxsrv on first launch
because the server listens on 6000 on every interface. Blocked is correct --
the guest reaches it over the host's own loopback, which slirp's rewrite
produces and the firewall does not police -- so the installer establishes that
state rather than asking the user to decide it:

    netsh firewall add allowedprogram program="...\vcxsrv.exe" name="VcXsrv" mode=DISABLE


9. Uninstall
-------------

    refuse if a guest is running, and say how to stop it
    stop the service, wait for STOPPED, delete it
    remove the firewall entry, the shortcuts, the program directory
    ask about root.img, defaulting to keeping it

The image is the user's data -- their installed packages, their files -- and it
is the one thing here that cannot be recreated by downloading the installer
again. Removing it by default because it is large is the wrong way round.

Never taskkill anything, in the uninstaller or anywhere else. taskkill on a
thread inside the driver closes its handles underneath kernel-mode code, and
that is the recurring bugcheck 0x50 in this port's history.


